UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 30, Lesson 2: Unit review and showcase', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A safety showcase brings together road safety, water and sun safety, spotting safe and unsafe situations, safety networks, online safety and safety at home and school. Present with checklists, role plays or posters and give reasons. Respect everyone''s experiences: share only what is comfortable and never ask others to tell personal stories.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWrite one sentence saying why having several people in your safety network is better than having one.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''If one person is not available or does not listen, I can tell another, so I always have a way to get help.'' It gives a reason that links to the idea of keep telling.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving that safety rules are only for little kids. People of all ages use safety rules to look after themselves and others.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCreate a safety showcase for a younger child covering road, water, sun, online and help-seeking safety with one checklist and one role play. Present it to a family member, collect two stars and a wish and add one improvement.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"A safety showcase brings together road safety, water and sun safety, spotting safe and unsafe situations, safety networks, online safety and safety at home and school. Present with checklists, role plays or posters and give reasons. Respect everyone''s experiences: share only what is comfortable and never ask others to tell personal stories.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Practise and apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-IHW-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Create a safety showcase for a younger child covering road, water, sun, online and help-seeking safety with one checklist and one role play. Present it to a family member, collect two stars and a wish and add one improve Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Sample: ''If one person is not available or does not listen, I can tell another, so I always have a way to get help.'' It gives a reason that links to the idea of keep telling. Reasoning: I used a showcase by attending to safety checklist rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Believing that safety rules are only for little kids. People of all ages use safety rules to look after themselves and others. Correction: Sample: ''If one person is not available or does not listen, I can tell another, so I always have a way to get help.'' It gives a reason that links to the idea… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a showcase.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing that safety rules are only for little kids. People of all ages use safety rules to look after themselves and others. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a showcase on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a showcase, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Sample: ''If one person is not available or does not listen, I can tell another, so I always have a way to get help.'' It gives a reason that links to the idea… This matches the lesson skill for a showcase.","options":["Believing that safety rules are only for little kids. People of all ages use safety rules to look after themselves and others.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Sample: ''If one person is not available or does not listen, I can tell another, so I always have a way to get help.'' It gives a reason that links to the idea…"],"question":"Write one sentence saying why having several people in your safety network is better than having one. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Believing that safety rules are only for little kids. People of all ages use safety rules to look after themselves and others. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["A safety showcase brings together road safety, water and sun safety, spotting safe and unsafe situations, safety networks, online safety and safety at home…","Sample: ''If one person is not available or does not listen, I can tell another, so I always have a way to get help.'' It gives a reason that links to the idea…","Careful use of safety checklist with clear evidence.","Believing that safety rules are only for little kids. People of all ages use safety rules to look after themselves and others."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named safety checklist, not by copying the model.","options":["Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the safety checklist used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right."],"question":"A learner is practising a showcase. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Sample: ''If one person is not available or does not listen, I can tell another, so I always have a way to get help.'' It gives a reason that links to the idea… uses safe, healthy, respectful choices appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Believing that safety rules are only for little kids. People of all ages use safety rules to look after themselves and others.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Sample: ''If one person is not available or does not listen, I can tell another, so I always have a way to get help.'' It gives a reason that links to the idea…"],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about a showcase.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Believing that safety rules are only for little kids. People of all ages use safety rules to look after themselves and others."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about safety checklist?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Example: Hana presents a ''Safety Hand'' poster and a checklist. Road: stop, look, listen, think. Water: flags and an adult. Sun: five Ss.…","Believing that safety rules are only for little kids. People of all ages use safety rules to look after themselves and others.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — A safety showcase brings together road safety, water and sun safety, spotting safe and unsafe situations,… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Copy the example and call that independent work.","Believing that safety rules are only for little kids. People of all ages use safety rules to look after themselves and others.","Create a safety showcase for a younger child covering road, water, sun, online and help-seeking safety with one checklist and one role play. Present…","Retype the worked example with no new example."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a showcase on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Sample: ''If one person is not available or does not listen, I can tell another, so I always have a way to get help.'' It gives a reason that links to the idea of keep telling. Reasoning: I used a showcase by attending to safety checklist rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with a showcase, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Believing that safety rules are only for little kids. People of all ages use safety rules to look after themselves and others. Correction: Sample: ''If one person is not available or does not listen, I can tell another, so I always have a way to get help.'' It gives a reason that links to the idea… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a showcase.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w30-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a showcase.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Healthy, Safe and Active Lifestyles","success_criteria":["I can use a showcase with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify safety checklist and explain why it matters.","I can practise safety network independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a reason needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A safety showcase brings together road safety, water and sun safety, spotting safe and unsafe situations, safety networks, online safety and safety at home and school. Present with checklists, role plays or posters and give reasons. Respect everyone''s experiences: share only…","options":["A guess with no reason.","A safety showcase brings together road safety, water and sun safety, spotting safe and unsafe situations, safety networks, online safety and safety at home and school. Present with checklists, role plays or posters and give reasons. Respect everyone''s experiences: share only what is comfortable and never ask others to tell personal stories.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Hana presents a ''Safety Hand'' poster and a checklist. Road: stop, look, listen, think. Water: flags and an adult. Sun: five Ss. Online: no personal information. Help: five trusted people. She ends with ''If I feel unsafe, I say No, go and tell.''\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite one sentence saying why having several people in your safety network is better than having one.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''If one person is not available or does not listen, I can tell another, so I always have a way to get help.'' It gives a reason that links to the idea of keep telling.","explain":"A safety showcase brings together road safety, water and sun safety, spotting safe and unsafe situations, safety networks, online safety and safety at home and school. Present with checklists, role plays or posters and give reasons. Respect everyone''s experiences: share only what is comfortable and never ask others to tell personal stories.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing that safety rules are only for little kids. People of all ages use safety rules to look after themselves and others. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"A safety showcase.","caption":"Cover every topic.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Topics</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">road, water, sun, online</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Help</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">five trusted people</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Hana presents a ''Safety Hand'' poster and a checklist. Road: stop, look, listen, think. Water: flags and an adul Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Hana presents a ''Safety Hand'' poster and a checklist. Road: stop, look, listen, think. Water: flags and an adul Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Hana presents a ''Safety Hand'' poster and a checklist. Road: stop, look, listen, think. Water: flags and an adult. Sun: five Ss. Online: no personal information. Help: five trusted people. She ends with ''If I feel unsafe, I say No, go and tell.'' Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"A closing message.","caption":"End with an action.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Poster</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Safety Hand</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Ending</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">say no, go, tell</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving that safety rules are only for little kids. People of all ages use safety rules to look after themselves and others. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Hana presents a ''Safety Hand'' poster and a checklist. Road: stop, look, listen, think. Water: flags and an adult. Sun: five Ss. Online: no personal information. Help: five trusted people. She ends with ''If I feel unsafe, I say No, go and tell.''\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''If one person is not available or does not listen, I can tell another, so I always have a way to get help.'' It gives a reason that links to the idea of keep telling.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Believing that safety rules are only for little kids. People of all ages use safety rules to look after themselves and others. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA safety showcase brings together road safety, water and sun safety, spotting safe and unsafe situations, safety networks, online safety and safety at home and school. Present with checklists, role plays or posters and give reasons. Respect everyone''s experiences: share only what is comfortable and never ask others to tell personal stories. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 30, Lesson 2: Unit review and showcase","unit_number":6,"unit_title":"Staying safe","week":30,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Hana presents a ''Safety Hand'' poster and a checklist. Road: stop, look, listen, think. Water: flags and an adult. Sun: five Ss. Online: no personal information. Help: five trusted people. She ends with ''If I feel unsafe, I say No, go and tell.''\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A safety showcase.","caption":"Cover every topic.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Topics</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">road, water, sun, online</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Help</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">five trusted people</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"A closing message.","caption":"End with an action.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Poster</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Safety Hand</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Ending</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">say no, go, tell</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-pdhpe-y3-w30-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
